<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">
Patient-Zero:
A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free 
<br class="ltx_break">Patient Agent Generation
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yunghwei Lai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Dept. of Comp. Sci. &amp; Tech., Institute for AI, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">11pt10ptmawz@tsinghua.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weizhi Ma
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute for AI Industry Research (AIR), Tsinghua University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">liuyang2011@tsinghua.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yang Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Dept. of Comp. Sci. &amp; Tech., Institute for AI, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute for AI Industry Research (AIR), Tsinghua University, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic data generation using large language models (LLMs) has emerged as a promising solution across various domains, particularly in medical field, to mitigate data collection challenges.
However, existing studies mainly utilize LLMs to rewrite and complete existing medical records, where the limitations in data privacy, accuracy, and diversity still exist, and additionally lack the ability to interact like real patients.
To address these issues, we propose a realistic patient generation framework, <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span>, which requires no real medical records. <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> first introduces a medically-aligned multi-step generation architecture, which builds comprehensive patient records through hierarchical medical knowledge injection without real medical records.
Then, to optimize the virtual patient’s interaction abilities with humans, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> designs a dynamic updating mechanism to improve the consistency and conversational performance.
Our framework enables the generation of contextually diverse patient records while maintaining strict medical coherence, supported by adaptive dialogue strategies and real-time clinical plausibility verification.
Experimental results demonstrate that our model achieves good performance in accuracy, diversity, and consistency. After training with our generated virtual patients, existing models show significant improvements on the MedQA dataset.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></p>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding authors.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11078v1/flowchart.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The proposed framework consists of two main modules: the Patient Record Construction module and the Patient Agent Interaction Simulation module. Patient records are generated through a multi-step process with atomic fact decomposition, using a knowledge base and stored in patient agent memory. In the Patient Agent Interaction Simulation module, these patient records are utilized to simulate dialogues between patient agents and doctors. Patient responses are evaluated using a triplet evaluation mechanism, where consistent responses are replied directly, neutral statements dynamically update the patient agent memory, and contradictory responses trigger regeneration.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown powerful content generation capabilities in various challenging language tasks. Beyond using them to answer questions, recent studies also use them to generate synthetic data across various domains for various purposes, e.g., model training and data construction. The high-quality data generated by LLMs can alleviate the time, labor, and financial costs associated with collecting real-world data. Due to privacy issues in the medical domain, using LLMs to generate medical-related data has become a promising direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing patient record generation approaches employ diverse methodologies to balance privacy protection with clinical utility.
Synthetic EHR generation is formulated as a probabilistic graphical model framework to generate high-quality medical reports <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Retrieval-Reasoning few-shot framework leverages LLMs to generate synthetic clinical trial data by retrieving relevant trials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. This approach generates new trials that mimic real-world data while preserving patient privacy.
Historical diagnostic information is used to guide LLMs in generating accurate radiology reports that reflect disease progression over time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Das et al. (2024)</a></cite> and text-to-tabular <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tornqvist et al. (2024)</a></cite> approaches are used to generate synthetic clinical trial data, improving the utility for downstream tasks and overcoming privacy concerns in clinical data sharing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, existing studies on medical data generation still rely on real medical records, which may cause the data privacy, accuracy, and diversity issues.
Firstly, existing studies have utilized real medical records as references in record generation. Although prompts may be designed to exclude personal patient information during the generation process, the uniqueness of the generated content may still pose a risk of privacy leakage and may also lead to false positives, meaning the generated data may not always fully adhere to medical principles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Secondly, the diversity and quality of synthetic medical data remain insufficient to fully capture the complexity and richness of real-world data, leading to biases or inaccuracies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Furthermore, how to enable synthetic patient records to possess conversation abilities like real patients remains a challenge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose a medically-aligned multi-step generation framework, which is named <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> due to no real-world medical records is used in patient generation and playing. <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> improves the quality of synthetic patient records and integrates them into realistic patient agent interactions using diverse conversational styles and real-time triplet evaluation mechanisms. This approach mimics the real-world hospital workflow, ensuring that generated data aligns with the step-by-step processes followed during patient diagnosis and treatment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To be more specific, by incorporating domain-specific knowledge, epidemiological considerations, and standardized examination protocols, <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> not only improves the diagnostic accuracy of generated data but also ensures alignment with established medical theories and practices.
The multi-step process generates an outline of core medical concepts, followed by patient details such as symptoms and demographics, and completes with detailed examination results aligned with medical guidelines.
The triplet evaluation mechanism was designed to ensure that the patient agent’s responses remain consistent with its underlying patient record, dynamically updating the patient agent memory or regenerating responses when contradictions arise.
We synthesized tens of thousands of medical records across multiple clinical departments and nearly a hundred diseases. Evaluation results indicate that the synthetic medical records exhibit high accuracy and consistency. In external validation, the generated generated medical records has been shown to enhance the model’s medical decision-making ability, demonstrating promising application potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our key contributions include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A multi-step generation framework aligns with medical principles is proposed in <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> to improve the accuracy and diversity of synthetic patient records.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> designed a patient-agent interaction framework with real-time agent memory management, which enable the virtual patients provide consistent conversation ability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comprehensive internal and external experiments demonstrated the prior performance of the generated medical records on accuracy, diversity, and consistency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patient Record Generation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the healthcare sector, electronic health records (EHRs) are commonly used to store patient information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kruse et al. (2017)</a></cite>.
The availability of information from electronic health records has significantly advanced medical research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goncalves et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
generative adversarial networks (GANs) were used to generate synthetic data for diverse applications, including drug discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Macedo et al. (2024)</a></cite>, medical imaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and privacy-preserving health monitoring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lange et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Retrieval-Reasoning few-shot framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a></cite> leverages large language models to generate synthetic clinical trials by combining retrieval of relevant trials.
AIPatient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2024)</a></cite> generates synthetic patient data using an EHR-constructed Knowledge Graph and a Reasoning Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework.
SynRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Das et al. (2024)</a></cite> uses reinforcement learning to align synthetic clinical trial data generation with human-preferred clinical endpoints.
Our work eliminates reliance on real patient data, introducing a hierarchical generation strategy that synthesizes accurate, diverse, and privacy-preserving medical records from scratch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patient Interaction Simulation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs create simulated clinical scenarios that replicate real-world patient agent interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lizée et al. (2024)</a></cite>, integrating patient’s information to evaluate diagnostic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
By leveraging EHRs and RAG, AIPatient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2024)</a></cite> creates personalized patient interactions through personality modeling and structured knowledge integration.
EvoPatient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2024)</a></cite> utilizes a coevolution mechanism to enhance the adaptability of simulated patients by iteratively refining responses based on the extracted high-quality examples from curated dialogues.
PSYCHE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduces a multi-faceted framework for simulating psychiatric patients, built upon a detailed psychiatric construct.
Cognitive biases in clinical decision-making can be mitigated by re-evaluating and correcting misconceptions through simulated multi-agent conversation framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.
Existing methods lack multidimensional patient persona construction with adaptive interaction capabilities in medical scene. Our work integrates structured patient record generation with a memory-based update system for realistic patient interactions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Patient-Zero</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> framework mainly consists of two parts: 1) Generating the virtual patient records from scratch but in a medically-aligned multi-step manner (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Medically-Aligned Multi-step Generation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), and 2) Using the generated patient records to construct interactive patient agents (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Patient Agent Interaction Simulation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Medically-Aligned Multi-step Generation</h3>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11078v1/generation_step.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Our multi-step approach contains three distinct stages: 1) generating an outline of the selected disease from the knowledge base, 2) creating basic patient information based on the outline, and 3) generating detailed examination results aligned with basic information and disease outline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing with previous medical record rewriting and completing methods, our <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> utilize only disease related knowledge to generate medical records. Though no privacy information is needed, it is more challenging to construct a comprehensive patient records from scratch, where the patient’s basic information, epidemiology, symptoms, and clinical examination results should be included.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve accurate and diverse patient records generation, we firstly propose a medically-aligned multi-step generation strategy, which is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Medically-Aligned Multi-step Generation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Note that the disease knowledge base can be easily collected from various websites (e.g., Wikipedia<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.wikipedia.org/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.wikipedia.org/</a></span></span></span>, Baidu Health Encyclopedia<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://jiankang.baidu.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://jiankang.baidu.com/</a></span></span></span>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 1: Disease Outline Selection.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before generating a specific synthetic medical record, we first select a target disease through a two-step process: choosing a medical department, then selecting a specific disease.
To avoid inconsistencies from varied formats and irrelevant information in direct disease knowledge, we human-verify and reconstruct the disease knowledge into an outline for generating diverse patient records.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Medically-Aligned Multi-step Generation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> Step 1, the outline includes demographic and disease-specific contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 2: Basic Information Generation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly prompting LLMs for complete records can result in overly long, inconsistent, and less diverse outputs. To address this, we use a multi-step approach based on medical logic, first generating basic patient inforamtion, and then clinical exam results.
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> generates patient’s basic information by a one-shot prompt with disease outline, as indicated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Medically-Aligned Multi-step Generation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Symptom trajectories are modeled via temporal probability distributions, capturing onset patterns, exacerbation triggers, and severity dynamics. These fundamental data are designed to reflect diverse demographics, ensuring that the synthetic patient records represent a wide range of patient populations. The full features can be seen in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Patient Case Studies ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 3: Detailed Information Generation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the final phase, clinical examination results are generated based on the patient’s symptoms and disease outline. A carefully selected one-shot prompt guides the generation of complex medical data while maintaining diversity. Each output is cross-referenced with prior information to ensure coherence throughout the synthetic patient record.
After the mentioned steps, we can obtain a full synthetic patient record, as shown in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Synthetic Patient Record ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Note that since our focus here is on the language models, hence only textual content without images is generated, which will be addressed in our future work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11078v1/atomic_statement.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The complete flow of interaction simulation begins with decomposing synthetic patient record into atomic statements, and a triplets evaluation is proposed to dynamic update the memory of the generated patient agent.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Patient Agent Interaction Simulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generated medical records can serve multiple purposes. We propose designing them to enhance the simulation of real patient interactions, enabling conversations with doctors or other roles (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Conversation ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). Thus, we introduce a novel patient agent interaction strategy with virtual memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The patient agent interaction framework simulates dynamic clinical dialogues through context-aware exchanges. The generated patient record serves as basic information for the patient role, stored as private memory. We propose dynamically updating the patient agent’s memory during conversations, integrating new, non-conflicting information while maintaining alignment with clinical details.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patient Agent Memory Construction.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by MediQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, we decompose the complete patient record into atomic statements stored in personal memory. These atomic statements are the smallest information units extracted from the complex record, standardizing the information into a simple, unified structure. This enables LLMs to process and reason over complex medical data more accurately and efficiently, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Step 3: Detailed Information Generation. ‣ 3.1 Medically-Aligned Multi-step Generation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The modular structure of atomic statements also allows seamless integration of new data into the patient agent memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To replicate human patient variability, our framework includes six conversational styles: plain, upset, verbose, reserved, tangent, and pleasing. These styles follow the taxonomy in PATIENT-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, allowing the generated patient agent to exhibit diverse communication behaviors. For instance, reserved patients give brief answers, while verbose ones provide excessive details. This variety enhances simulation depth, helping medical trainees adapt to different patient behaviors and better prepare for real clinical encounters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lizée et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Triplets Evaluation for Memory Updating.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework further implements a triplet evaluation mechanism to ensure the accuracy and coherence of the patient agent’s responses. The system cross-references each response against the patient’s record to identify and address inconsistencies. Contradictions trigger iterative regeneration until alignment is achieved. Furthermore, the system dynamically updates patient records by integrating new clinical information while ensuring conflict resolution. This adaptive memory mechanism mirrors the evolution of real clinical dialogues while preventing data corruption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The triplet evaluation mechanism for assessing patient responses can be mathematically formalized as follows.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the response generated by the patient agent, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the decomposed patient record, which consists of a set of atomic facts <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{F_{1},F_{2},\ldots,F_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F_{1},F_{2},\ldots,F_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. To evaluate the relationship between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and a specific fact <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}\in P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}\in P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define an evaluation function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> that quantifies their alignment.
The function maps to one of the three discrete labels: <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)\in\{\mathcal{E}\ \text{(Entail)},\ \mathcal{N}\ \text{(Neutral)},\ \mathcal{C}\ \text{(Contradict)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(Entail)</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(Neutral)</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(Contradict)</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)\in\{\mathcal{E}\ \text{(Entail)},\ \mathcal{N}\ \text{(Neutral)},\ \mathcal{C}\ \text{(Contradict)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, the evaluation function is:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)=\begin{cases}\mathcal{E},&amp;\text{if }R_{p}\models F_{i},\\
\mathcal{C},&amp;\text{if }R_{p}\models\neg F_{i},\\
\mathcal{N},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊧</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊧</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)=\begin{cases}\mathcal{E},&amp;\text{if }R_{p}\models F_{i},\\
\mathcal{C},&amp;\text{if }R_{p}\models\neg F_{i},\\
\mathcal{N},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\models" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊧</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\models</annotation></semantics></math> denotes logical entailment.
Algorithm 1 shows the interaction steps between the generated patient agent with human/AI doctors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The patient agent uses LLM automated validation to manage memory updates. Consistent responses are sent to doctors without updates, while neutral responses trigger memory updates if verified as non-contradictory and complementary to existing records. Contradictory responses are regenerated until they align with documented facts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The agent memory update actions are taken based on the evaluation label. If <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tri(R_{p},F_{i})=\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tri(R_{p},F_{i})=\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, the response <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is accepted as accurate that will be sent to the doctor, and no update will be conducted. If <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tri(R_{p},F_{i})=\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tri(R_{p},F_{i})=\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the new information <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> only if it satisfies both <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;">universal local neutrality</span> (neutral to <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;">all</span> existing facts in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;">global consistency</span> (no contradiction with any fact in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>). In cases where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)=\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tri\left(R_{p},F_{i}\right)=\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, the patient agent will regenerate the response.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Interaction Simulation Framework</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
Initialize patient record <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}\leftarrow\{F_{1},F_{2},\ldots,F_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}\leftarrow\{F_{1},F_{2},\ldots,F_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <em id="alg1.4.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interaction is not complete</em> <span id="alg1.4.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">repeat</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
              Generate patient response <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{p}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{p}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
              contradictions <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text">false</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="alg1.4.6.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fact <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}\in P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}\in P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="alg1.4.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
              <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="result\leftarrow\textsc{Tri}\left(R_{p}^{(k)},F_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Tri</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">result\leftarrow\textsc{Tri}\left(R_{p}^{(k)},F_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.4.7.2" class="ltx_text">via (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Triplets Evaluation for Memory Updating. ‣ 3.2 Patient Agent Interaction Simulation ‣ 3 Patient-Zero ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
