<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Performance comparison of synthetic data generation methods across different models, evaluated using Direct Generation, <span id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span> without outline (Patient-Zero <sub id="S4.T3.7" class="ltx_sub">w/o outline</sub>), and <span id="S4.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patient-Zero</span>. Bold values indicate the best performance per model group; underlined values represent the highest overall scores.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Performance of Generated Records ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the impact of direct generation versus our multi-step generation strategies, with and without outlines, across different LLM backbones.
Patient-Zero consistently outperforms other approaches, achieving the highest accuracy and the lowest similarity scores.
The capability of the model matters, as different LLMs exhibit varying performance gains from multi-step refinement.
Multi-step generation without outline (Patient-Zero <sub id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub">w/o outline</sub>) underperforms compared to the outlined version across all metrics, highlighting the importance of explicit hierarchical structuring for coherent clinical data synthesis.
Direct generation struggles with diversity, indicating a tendency towards verbatim replication.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Performance of Patient Agent Interaction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we randomly selected 10 generated patients from each department for evaluation (60 patients in total). Each patient participated in two types of dialogue sessions: cross-dialogue and single dialogue. In the cross-dialogue setting, each patient underwent two rounds of dialogue, with a total of 13 questions asked per round by a doctor agent, totalling 1,560 dialogues. The single dialogue setting involved only one round of conversation per patient, totalling 780 dialogues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compared different input formats, including structured JSON (for hierarchical data), unstructured text (mimicking clinical notes) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Irugalbandara (2024)</a></cite>, with our atomic patient agent memory to evaluate the quality of patient agent interaction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:201.3pt;vertical-align:-97.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(25.8pt,-15.1pt) scale(1.1760152581888,1.1760152581888) ;">
