<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Comparison of interaction quality across different representation formats, evaluating JSON, plain text, and Ours for both single-dialogue and cross-dialogue interactions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Experimental results in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3 Performance of Patient Agent Interaction ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show that our method outperforms the other three conversational scenarios across all metrics. Our structured memory architecture significantly improves over the baselines, enhancing the alignment of patient responses and emotions with their records and predefined styles. It also generates more coherent and natural responses, improving overall interaction quality. Notably, incorporating memory updates in cross-dialogue interactions leads to a significant improvement in performance, strengthening role-playing capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>External Evaluation</h3>

