<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Patient Case Studies</h2>

<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11078v1/data_comparison.svg" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Comparison of direct generation and multi-step generation approaches for Sciatica patient cases. The direct generation method yields basic, less detailed cases, whereas the multi-step approach progressively refines details, enhancing granularity and realism.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A Patient Case Studies ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the direct generation method generates basic case patient records but lacks depth and precision. Symptoms like "pain, tingling, numbness, and difficulty walking" are noted without specifying location, severity, or triggers. Duration is limited to "4 weeks", with no mention of fluctuation or aggravating factors. Epidemiological data is superficial; a "sedentary lifestyle due to a desk job" offers no meaningful insight, while family history is erroneously marked as "None", ignoring hereditary factors. Examination results contain incorrect information, such as a "positive straight leg raising test at 60 degrees" (a value inconsistent with standard nerve tension tests), and imaging findings vaguely mention "compression at L4-L5 vertebrae" without detailing structural abnormalities or nerve involvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Symptoms are described comprehensively using the multi-step method, alongside contextual details like fluctuating intensity and responses to stretching. Epidemiological data incorporates a history of lumbar disc herniation, occasional exercise habits, and a BMI of 29, highlighting lifestyle and genetic risk factors. Family history correctly identifies a father with chronic back problems and a maternal uncle with herniated discs, offering valuable hereditary context. Examination findings are accurate and clinically relevant: a "positive straight leg raising test at 45 degrees" aligns with severe nerve root tension, while imaging identifies a "moderate protrusion of the L4-L5 disc compressing the left L5 nerve root". Electrophysiology confirms "denervation in muscles innervated by the L5 root", ruling out significant long-term damage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Synthetic Patient Record</h2>

<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><span id="A2.F5.2" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A2.F5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Basic Information</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ID:</span> 10024
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Name:</span> Clara Gutierrez
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gender:</span> Female
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Age:</span> 47
<br class="ltx_break"></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Epidemiology</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medical History:</span> Chronic pancreatitis diagnosed 5 years ago, history of gallstones, high cholesterol
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lifestyle Factor:</span> Moderate alcohol consumption (social drinker), high-fat diet, sedentary lifestyle, former smoker (quit 2 years ago)
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vaccination History:</span> Not vaccinated for hepatitis A or B
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Family History:</span> Mother had gallbladder issues, father had pancreatitis and diabetes
<br class="ltx_break"></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Disease Information</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disease:</span> Pancreatitis
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Level:</span> Severe
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Symptoms:</span> Acute abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, fever, dyspnea, hypotension, upper abdominal distension, elevated blood sugar
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F5.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Duration:</span> Symptoms have been present for the last 10 days, worsening over the past 48 hours, with severe abdominal pain radiating to the back.
<br class="ltx_break"></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.F5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Examination Results</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Routine Blood Test:</span> Serum amylase level is significantly elevated at 450 U/L (normal range: 30-110 U/L) and lipase at 900 U/L (normal range: 0-160 U/L). Blood glucose is also elevated at 220 mg/dL, indicating possible pancreatic damage affecting insulin secretion.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Biochemical Test:</span> The white blood cell count is elevated at 14,000 cells/mm0̆0b3, suggesting an inflammatory response. Liver function tests show mild elevation in ALT at 60 U/L (normal range: 7-56 U/L) and alkaline phosphatase at 150 U/L (normal range: 44-147 U/L), indicating possible biliary involvement. Kidney function tests remain within normal limits.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Imaging Tests:</span> CT scan of the abdomen reveals significant pancreatic edema with peritoneal fluid collection and a 3 cm pseåudocyst formation adjacent to the pancreas. No evidence of necrosis is noted. An abdominal ultrasound confirms the presence of gallstones in the gallbladder, with a dilated common bile duct measuring 10 mm.</span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>A synthetic patient medical record generated for a severe Pancreatitis case.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This case in Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Synthetic Patient Record ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents a patient with severe acute pancreatitis and multiple risk factors. The patient has a complex medical history, including chronic pancreatitis, gallstones, and hypercholesterolemia, compounded by an unhealthy lifestyle (high-fat diet and alcohol consumption). Imaging reveals pancreatic edema with pseudocyst formation and biliary abnormalities (gallstones with common bile duct dilation), suggesting biliary pancreatitis. Laboratory findings indicate significantly elevated pancreatic enzyme levels, inflammatory response, and glucose metabolism disorders, consistent with pancreatic parenchymal injury. A family history of pancreatic diseases and metabolic disorders warrants attention, while the lack of hepatitis vaccination may increase the risk of secondary infections. The disease course shows acute exacerbation, necessitating vigilance for potential progression from local complications to systemic multi-organ dysfunction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Conversation</h2>

<figure id="A3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11078v1/doctor_patient_conversation.png" id="A3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Example of a patient agent conversational interaction where the patient has Social Anxiety Disorder. The dialogue demonstrates the patient’s responses to the doctor’s questions about age, allergies, relationship status, chronic illnesses, daily life challenges, and environmental factors. The interaction highlights the patient’s struggles with anxiety, depression, and social functioning, providing a realistic and empathetic representation of their condition.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in Figure <a href="#A3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix C Conversation ‣ Patient-Zero: A Unified Framework for Real-Record-Free Patient Agent Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, a 24-year-old patient with Social Anxiety Disorder (SAD) and comorbid depressive symptoms has experienced persistent anxiety since age 22, leading to severe social withdrawal and functional impairment. The patient exhibits cognitive distortions (fear of judgment) and behavioral avoidance, reinforcing isolation and distress. Daily activities, including work and errands, are disrupted, with crowded environments exacerbating symptoms. The presence of recurrent depressive episodes suggests potential Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) or anxiety-induced depression, requiring further assessment. Key risk factors include lack of social support and chronic avoidance. The doctor’s empathetic approach highlights the need for a structured treatment plan, such as CBT, SSRIs, and social skills training, to address both anxiety and depression while promoting gradual social reintegration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
